    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d77">

                <pb facs="0081" n="67" xml:id="pg67"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851G.711/1–2547: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-01-25T12:00:00+01:00">January 25,
                            1947—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>336. Question of censorship in Indochina discussed with Baudet<anchor n="43"
                        xml:id="d77-f1"/> last evening. He said that a message had been sent to
                    d’Argenlieu pointing out the possible injurious effect on American public
                    opinion of censorship policy recently adopted. Baudet added, however, that
                    Foreign Office did not feel it had the authority to issue instructions to
                    d’Argenlieu on a matter which is considered to be a military question.<anchor
                        n="44" xml:id="d77-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d77-f1" n="43" xml:id="d77-f1-content">
                <p>Philippe Baudet, Director of the Asiatic Division of the French Foreign
                    Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d77-f2" n="44" xml:id="d77-f2-content">
                <p>Despatch 187, February 19, from Saigon, gave a detailed account of steps taken
                    since mid-November to control the dissemination of information in Indochina and
                    the flow of information out of Indochina. As the Chief of the Division of
                    Southeast Asian Affairs (Moffat) commented in a memorandum of March 7, “the
                    picture of the censorship exercised by the French as it appears in this despatch
                    is that of a totalitarian regime at its worst.” The Department on April 28
                    commended the writer of the despatch for compiling “this discerning and
                    comprehensive report on a subject of the greatest interest to the Department.”
                    (851G.918/2–1947)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
